{"id": "sllama0188", "pool": "sllama", "arm": "balanced", "tier": "balanced", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 2121542183, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "f40edaa94b82cc480a8162abcd570effa331c0a9b7ca31632511f1cd5b9e06ff", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.7972545623779297, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 0, "query": "Who is the supreme god in Hinduism?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/sllama0188.wav", "input_sha256": "e208704941da39600e137df1aa4361046c9b4781f45f77111f3ed65e119eb4df", "input_samples": 43096, "input_s": 2.693, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.3209, "act": 0.9996, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "In Hinduism, there isn' t a single supreme god like in some mono theistic religions. Instead, it's more about different de ities representing various aspect s of the divine. One of the most important figures is Bra hman, which is considered the ultimate reality or the source of all existence. It' s not a person, but rather an unman ifested absolute. Then you have gods and goddesses who are seen as manifestation s of Brah man. For example, B rama is often considered one of the three primary creator gods, along with Vishnu and S iv a. Vi shnu is known for maintaining order and duality, while Siva represents transformation and destruction that leads to renewal. So, while there's no one \"s upreme\" god in the traditional sense, these deities are all part of the same ultimate reality, just expressed differently depending on what people need or want to focus on.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 48, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": 